# Project Rules for AI-Assisted Development

> For modular rules, see `.cursor/rules/` directory.

## Technologies
- Python 3.12
- Package manager: uv
- FastAPI for REST APIs
- SQLAlchemy for ORM
- Pydantic for validation
- Ruff for linting and formatting

## Patterns
- Use dependency injection
- Separate business logic from controllers
- Validate inputs with Pydantic schemas
- Follow repository pattern for data access
- Use service layer for business logic

## Code Language
- All code, comments, docstrings, logs, and error messages MUST be in English
- Variable names, function names, class names: use English
- Comments and docstrings: write in English
- Log messages and exceptions: in English
- Technical documentation: in English

## File Naming
- File and directory names must be in lowercase with underscores for separation
- Documentation files: use lowercase (e.g., git_conventions.md)
- Code files: follow Python conventions (snake_case)
- Examples: `user_service.py`, `api_endpoints/`, `database_models.py`

## File Generation Rules
- DO NOT automatically generate README.md files
- DO NOT include icons/emojis in code, logs, or documentation
- Use logging instead of print statements
- Only create documentation files when explicitly requested
- Prefer updating existing documentation over creating new files
- If new documentation is needed, ask the user first

## Logging
- Use logging.getLogger(__name__) instead of print()
- Do not use print() in production code
- Configure appropriate log levels (DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL)
- All log messages must be in English
- Use structured logging when possible
- DO NOT use f-strings with logging (use % formatting or extra parameter instead)
- Example: logger.info("User %s logged in", user_id) NOT logger.info(f"User {user_id} logged in")

## Package Manager
- Use uv to manage dependencies and virtual environments
- Use uv add to add new dependencies
- Use uv sync to install dependencies
- Do not use pip directly, use uv pip if necessary
- Lock dependencies with uv lock

## Code Quality
- Use type hints for all function parameters and return types
- Include docstrings for all public functions and classes (Google format)
- Write unit tests for new functionality
- Follow PEP 8 style guide
- Use Ruff for linting and formatting (line length: 100)

## Error Handling
- Use specific exceptions, not generic Exception
- Include descriptive error messages in English
- Log errors with appropriate context
- Do not silently catch and ignore exceptions

## Testing
- Write tests for all new features
- Use pytest as testing framework
- Aim for >80% code coverage
- Use fixtures for test data setup
- Test both happy paths and edge cases

## Memory Bank
- If the memory-bank/ directory exists:
  - Read ALL files at the start of a session
  - Update activeContext.md with current changes
  - Document technical decisions in systemPatterns.md
  - Keep progress.md updated
  - When "update memory bank" is requested, review all files

## Git Conventions
- Follow Conventional Commits format
- Include Jira ticket key in commit messages: [TICKET-123]
- Commits should be atomic and focused
- Write descriptive commit messages in English

## Code Organization
- Group related functionality in modules
- Use clear separation of concerns
- Keep functions focused and single-purpose
- Avoid deep nesting (max 3-4 levels)
- Use meaningful variable and function names
